1. Root cause: `matchConstraint` applies record-type constraints to any `PrototypeObjectType`, even though it should only refine anonymous object types, so it can wrongly mutate shared/non-anonymous object types by inventing inferred properties.

2. The patch changes this by returning early when the receiver is a native object type and the constraint is a record type; in this method, that effectively means: “if `this` is native, do nothing.”

3. That does not remove the condition in step 1. It removes only one subset of the bad cases: native object types. The underlying bug is broader: named, non-native `PrototypeObjectType`s are still mutated by record constraints.

4. Concrete remaining bad input:
   ```js
   /** @constructor */
   function Foo() {}

   /** @param {{x: string}} r */
   function needsX(r) {}

   /** @param {Foo} foo */
   function use(foo) {
     needsX(foo);
   }

   /** @param {Foo} foo */
   function read(foo) {
     return foo.x;
   }
   ```
   On the bad path, `matchConstraint` is invoked on the named instance type `Foo` with record constraint `{x:string}`. Because `Foo` is not native, the patch does not return early, so it still defines inferred property `x` on `Foo` (typically as `string|undefined` if absent). That is the same root-cause computation: a non-anonymous type is being structurally rewritten. The wrong value remains that `Foo` now reports a property type for `x` instead of remaining unchanged / absent.

5. So this patch fixes the reported symptom by blocking one specific source of pollution (native object types), but it does not fix the real cause described by the method’s own comment (“We only want to match constraints on anonymous types.”).

